  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Intermittent light rain mixed with snow, mainly\nnorth of Bangor, through today. Snow accumulations will be \nextremely minor, if any at all.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nAs the weak low pressure system exits to the E, remaining\nshortwave energy will help produce precip showers today. In\naddition, instability values will begin to increase this morning\nand continue through the afternoon with about 50 J/kg for CAPE\nand steepening lapse rates. Thus the showers will be convective.\nThe precip type will depend on temps in the north. Snow\naccumulation will be light, if any due to the warm surface\ntemps.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Potential for coastal storm development later in\nthe week. However, uncertainty still exists.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure will track from the Mid-Atlantic coast toward\nsouthern New England Thursday night. Current indications are\nthat this low then tracks east/northeast, to the south of the \nGulf of Maine, Friday while a secondary low develops in the \nwake of the initial low. Models have generally trended \nsouthward with this system. However, uncertainty still exists \nregarding the tracks of both the initial and secondary lows. The\ntrend southward has, for now, decreased the potential impacts \nof this system across the forecast area. Potential impacts from \nthis system, including precipitation types/amounts and possible \nsnow accumulations, from Thursday night into Saturday will be \ndependent on the eventual tracks of these lows.",
